    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> city-state on an island with a deep harbour, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold for Indian cloth and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1331; by 1500 it was among the largest and richest towns on the East African coast.</li><li>It resisted the Portuguese for nearly a century until they built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-jesus">Fort Jesus</a> and took control in 1593.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> noted that Mombasa’s people grew no grain on the island: they lived on bananas and fish and brought their grain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> mainland.</p></aside>
    </section>

