    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Idrisi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Muhammad al-Idrisi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_de_Al-Idrisi_bajo_el_baluarte_de_los_Mallorquines,_Ceuta_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Estatua_de_Al-Idrisi_bajo_el_baluarte_de_los_Mallorquines,_Ceuta_(3).jpg: Mario Sánchez Bueno derivative work: Ecemaml, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mombasa grew up on a coral island separated from the mainland of what is now Kenya by narrow creeks, which gave it one of the best natural harbours on the East African coast. Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns, it developed from a community of African farmers, fishers and traders who spoke an early form of Swahili, a Bantu language, and who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> carried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds. Its own people knew the town as Mvita, and later Swahili traditions name early rulers such as the queen Mwana Mkisi and Shehe Mvita, figures whose dates cannot be fixed. The geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a>, writing in the 1150s, mentions Mombasa as a small town on the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a>, and by the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries it had become a Muslim trading centre. The Moroccan traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who stopped there in 1331, described a large island town whose people were pious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims of the Shafi‘i school, with well-built wooden mosques, who lived on bananas and fish and imported their grain from the mainland. Mombasa competed for the coastal trade with Malindi to the north and Kilwa to the south, and like them it was governed by a Muslim ruling family drawing its wealth from commerce.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sofala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sofala.jpg" alt="Sofala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sofala1683.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mallet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mombasa lived by trade. Dhows sailing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, glassware and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, and took away <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold, ambergris, mangrove poles, rhinoceros horn and slaves. Much of the gold came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> and Kilwa, and Mombasa merchants also traded with the peoples of the hinterland, the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mijikenda">Mijikenda</a>, who supplied ivory, grain and other produce in exchange for cloth and beads. Society was divided between the patrician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> families who owned the stone houses, ships and plantations, and a much larger population of craftspeople, sailors, clients and slaves. Many patricians claimed Arab or Persian ‘Shirazi’ ancestry, which reinforced their status in an Islamic world, although their language and much of their culture were African. By around 1500 the town had grown considerably: the Portuguese writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa">Duarte Barbosa</a>, writing a few years after the first European contacts, described Mombasa as a large and handsome town of stone and mortar houses, with a busy harbour full of ships and an active trade with Sofala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Cambay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> shaped law, education and daily life, and mosques and tombs of coral stone stood among the houses of the old town.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="Vasco da Gama" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ignoto_portoghese,_ritratto_di_un_cavaliere_dell%27ordine_di_cristo,_1525-50_ca._02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mombasa’s defences relied on its island position, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and swordsmen of its citizens and on alliances with the peoples of the mainland, but it had no artillery or ocean-going warships to match the Portuguese. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> arrived in April 1498, the town’s rulers were hostile and attempted to seize his ships, and he sailed on to Mombasa’s rival Malindi, which welcomed him. In August 1505 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a>, on his way to become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, stormed Mombasa after fierce resistance, sacked and burned it and carried off a large booty; the ruler fled to the mainland. The town recovered and remained openly hostile, and in 1528 a Portuguese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-da-cunha">Nuno da Cunha</a> attacked and burned it again. In the 1580s Mombasa welcomed the Ottoman corsair <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-ali-bey">Mir Ali Bey</a>, whose expeditions encouraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns to throw off Portuguese overlordship. In 1589 a Portuguese fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> came to Mombasa to destroy him just as a mainland raiding force known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimba">Zimba</a> arrived on the shore. The Portuguese defeated and captured Mir Ali, and the Zimba crossed to the island and slaughtered many of its inhabitants, a blow from which independent Mombasa never recovered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mombasa was governed by a hereditary Muslim ruler, called <em>sultan</em> or <em>shaykh</em> in Arabic sources and <em>mfalme</em> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a>, drawn from the leading families of the town. His authority was limited by the merchant clans whose wealth sustained the city, and important decisions required consultation with their elders. The ruler controlled the port, received gifts and customs from visiting merchants, and conducted relations with other city-states and with the mainland peoples, whose cooperation was essential for food supplies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade. Islamic law, administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> and scholars, regulated commerce, marriage and inheritance, and the Friday mosque was the centre of civic as well as religious life. Later Swahili sources describe the town as divided into groups of clans, the confederations later known as the Three Tribes and the Nine Tribes, but these are best documented from the seventeenth century onwards, and how far they existed before 1593 is uncertain. Mombasa sometimes extended influence over smaller settlements on the nearby coast, but it never built a territorial state; its power lay in its harbour, its trade and its relationships. The names of most of its rulers before the Portuguese conquest are not reliably known.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fort-jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-jesus.jpg" alt="Fort Jesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fort-jesus">Fort Jesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Jesus_at_the_Mombasa_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maingi030, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mombasa was one of the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> city-states that made the East African coast a meeting place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its merchants helped carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold from the African interior to markets across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and its mosques, stone houses and literature formed part of the urban Islamic culture of the Swahili coast. It also stands out as the most persistent African opponent of the Portuguese in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Where Malindi chose alliance, Mombasa repeatedly refused to submit, and the Portuguese came to see its harbour as the key to controlling the coast north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. That judgement shaped the region’s later history: from 1593 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-jesus">Fort Jesus</a> at Mombasa became the headquarters of Portuguese power in East Africa, its capture by Omani forces in 1698 after a long siege ended Portuguese rule north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruvuma-river">Ruvuma</a>, and the town later became the base of the Mazrui governors and, under British rule, the starting point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-railway">Uganda Railway</a>. The long rivalry between Mombasa and Malindi also influenced the politics of the whole coast. Today Mombasa is Kenya’s second city and its main seaport, and its Old Town preserves the Swahili urban tradition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent city-state of Mombasa was destroyed by the combination of Portuguese naval power and the events of 1589. After the sackings of 1505 and 1528, the town had rebuilt and continued to trade, but it remained the centre of opposition to Portuguese overlordship. Its support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-ali-bey">Mir Ali Bey</a> in the 1580s brought the decisive Portuguese expedition of 1589, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimba">Zimba</a> massacre that followed left the town depopulated and defenceless. A few years later, around 1592, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segeju">Segeju</a>, a warlike people allied to Malindi, are said to have attacked Mombasa and killed its ruler. In 1593 the Portuguese, who had decided to build a permanent fortress on the coast, began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-jesus">Fort Jesus</a> on the island, designed by the Italian architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-cairato">Giovanni Battista Cairato</a>, and installed the ruler of Malindi as sultan of Mombasa under their protection. This ended Mombasa’s existence as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> city-state. Portuguese Mombasa lasted until 1698, apart from a rising in 1631 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-al-hasan">Yusuf bin Hasan</a> killed the Portuguese garrison. The island later passed to the Omani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, to the Mazrui governors and, in the nineteenth century, to the Sultanate of Zanzibar and British rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mombasa was one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> city-states of the East African coast, a wealthy Muslim port linking the African interior with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the most determined local opponent of the Portuguese, whose conquest of it in 1593 turned the island into their main stronghold.</em></p>

